{
  "analysis": "The terminal is at /app (working directory). There's a data processing pipeline with scripts: run_pipeline.sh, collect_data.sh, process_data.sh, generate_report.sh. The pipeline is failing. I need to inspect these scripts, identify issues, and fix them. The patch history hints suggest a prior fix was changing a shebang from #!/bin/nonexistent to #!/bin/bash and making scripts executable, plus chmod 755.",
  "plan": "First, list the directory and read all the scripts to understand the pipeline and find issues. I'll look at run_pipeline.sh to see how it invokes the others, then check shebangs, permissions, and any broken dependencies.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat run_pipeline.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat collect_data.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat process_data.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat generate_report.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I can see the issues:\n1. `generate_report.sh` has a broken shebang `#!/bin/nonexistent` — must be `#!/bin/bash`.\n2. `collect_data.sh` has permissions `--wx--x--x` (no read bit) — scripts need read to be parsed.\n3. `process_data.sh` is `-rw-r--r--` (no execute bit).\n4. `run_pipeline.sh` is `-rw-r--r--` (no execute bit).\n\nFixes needed: correct the shebang, and ensure all scripts are readable + executable.",
  "plan": "Fix the shebang in generate_report.sh with sed, then make all four scripts executable (chmod 755 gives read+write+execute). Then run the pipeline and verify it completes successfully.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -i '1s|#!/bin/nonexistent|#!/bin/bash|' generate_report.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "chmod 755 collect_data.sh process_data.sh generate_report.sh run_pipeline.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -1 generate_report.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "./run_pipeline.sh\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "collect_data.sh ran fine, but process_data.sh fails with 'cannot execute: required file not found'. This classic error happens when the script has a shebang with a carriage return (CRLF / Windows line endings), making the interpreter path `#!/bin/bash\\r` which doesn't exist. I need to check the line endings of process_data.sh.",
  "plan": "Inspect process_data.sh for carriage returns with `cat -A`. If CRLF is present, strip CR characters. Then re-run the pipeline. I'll also check the other scripts to be safe.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat -A process_data.sh | head -5\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat -A collect_data.sh | head -3\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Confirmed: process_data.sh has CRLF (^M) line endings. The shebang becomes `#!/bin/bash\\r` which the kernel can't find as an interpreter, causing 'cannot execute: required file not found'. collect_data.sh has clean LF endings. I'll strip carriage returns from all scripts to be safe.",
  "plan": "Use sed to remove trailing carriage returns from all four scripts, then re-run the pipeline to verify it completes successfully.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -i 's/\\r$//' collect_data.sh process_data.sh generate_report.sh run_pipeline.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "./run_pipeline.sh\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

